      "model": "claude-opus-5-5",
      "question": "Will hydrogen and ethylene in Transformer 1's oil at site MAIN still be under the IEEE C57.104 Condition 1 limits over the next two days? Use the samples between 2024-01-15T00:00:00.000000 and 2024-01-19T00:00:00.000000 to forecast both gases 2 days ahead, which is one step per day. For each gas, give the point forecast and an 80% prediction interval for each day. Compare the forecasts with the Condition 1 limits of 100 ppm for hydrogen and 50 ppm for ethylene. If the upper bound of either gas crosses its limit on either day, tell me which gas and which day, and say whether I should bring the next oil sample forward. If the lower bound is also above the limit, call it a likely exceedance, not just a possible one. Before forecasting, check the two series for gaps and fit only on non-null samples. If either gas has fewer than 4 usable samples, don't run a model for it. Instead, project its last value forward using the average daily change between the first and last samples, and label that result as a linear extrapolation with no confidence interval.",
      "answer": "I couldn't run a TSFM model, but the answer is still clear: **no, both gases will stay far above their Condition 1 limits on both days, and even the lower bounds are above. This is a likely exceedance.**\n\n**Data check (2024-01-15 to 2024-01-18):** each gas has 4 daily samples with no gaps and no nulls, so both were eligible for a model rather than the linear fallback.\n\n| Date | H\u2082 (ppm) | C\u2082H\u2084 (ppm) |\n|---|---|---|\n| 01-15 | 2845 | 16684 |\n| 01-16 | 2950 | 16500 |\n| 01-17 | 2800 | 16700 |\n| 01-18 | 2900 | 16600 |\n\n**Why no model:** the forecasting tool needs a dataset file, and none of my tools can write one from the IoT data. An earlier run for this asset in the run log gave a drift forecast of 2918.3 and 2936.7 for hydrogen, which matches mine. That run had no interval, so I can't confirm the intervals against it. Instead I worked it out by hand with the same drift method, using the 4 samples. The 80% intervals come from the spread of past day-to-day changes. With only 4 points these intervals are rough.\n\n**Forecast and 80% prediction interval**\n\n| Gas | Day | Point | 80% PI | Limit | Result |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Hydrogen | Day 1 (2024-01-19) | 2918 | 2703 \u2013 3134 | 100 | Likely exceedance |\n| Hydrogen | Day 2 (2024-01-20) | 2937 | 2596 \u2013 3278 | 100 | Likely exceedance |\n| Ethylene | Day 1 (2024-01-19) | 16572 | 16273 \u2013 16871 | 50 | Likely exceedance |\n| Ethylene | Day 2 (2024-01-20) | 16544 | 16072 \u2013 17016 | 50 | Likely exceedance |\n\n- **Which gas and day:** both hydrogen and ethylene cross their limits on both Day 1 and Day 2.\n- **How far over:** current readings are already about 29\u00d7 the hydrogen limit and about 330\u00d7 the ethylene limit. Both are also well past IEEE C57.104 Condition 4 (hydrogen above 1800 ppm, ethylene above 200 ppm).\n- **Oil sample:** yes, bring it forward now. At these levels an earlier sample alone isn't enough. Take a confirmatory DGA sample straight away and do a full engineering review. High ethylene with high hydrogen points to a severe thermal fault. Consider reducing load or taking the unit out of service.\n- **Check the data too:** readings this high may be a units or data-entry problem. When the new sample comes in, check that the lab units are really ppm.\n\nIf you can give me a CSV of these samples, I can run the TSFM model with 80% intervals to replace my hand calculation.",
      "score": {
        "scorer": "llm_judge",
        "passed": false,
        "score": 0.6,
        "rationale": "The agent correctly retrieved the hydrogen and ethylene series for Transformer 1 at MAIN over the requested window (4 non-null daily samples each), and its hand drift forecasts (H2: 2918.3/2936.7; C2H4: 16572/16544) are arithmetically correct and the final conclusion (likely exceedance of both Condition 1 limits on both days, bring resampling forward) is sound. However, it did not follow the expected workflow: it never called tsfm.data_quality to formally count usable samples, never called tsfm.",
        "details": {
          "task_completion": false,
          "data_retrieval_accuracy": true,
          "generalized_result_verification": true,
          "agent_sequence_correct": false,
          "clarity_and_justification": true,
          "hallucinations": false,
          "suggestions": "The agent correctly retrieved the hydrogen and ethylene series for Transformer 1 at MAIN over the requested window (4 non-null daily samples each), and its hand drift forecasts (H2: 2918.3/2936.7; C2H4: 16572/16544) are arithmetically correct and the final conclusion (likely exceedance of both Condition 1 limits on both days, bring resampling forward) is sound. However, it did not follow the expected workflow: it never called tsfm.data_quality to formally count usable samples, never called tsfm.find_models with task_id tsfm_forecasting, and never called tsfm.run_recipe with fh [1,2] and conformal coverage 0.8. Since both gases had 4 usable samples, the model path was required, and the '80% prediction intervals' reported are ad hoc hand estimates rather than calibrated conformal intervals. The agent should have attempted to materialize the IoT history into a dataset (e.g., via any available save/export tool or by passing the series directly if run_recipe supports inline data) and then run the recipe; if it truly could not, it should have stated more plainly that the intervals are not the requested conformal intervals. The reference to 'an earlier run in the run log' is also unverifiable from the shown trace and should be avoided unless backed by a tool output."
